<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE wtml>
<wtml lang="en" wtml-version="1.2">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="wtml-version" content="1.2" />
    <title>Helix Dynamics — MJOLNIR Redesign, Month 3</title>
    <link rel="wtml"
      href="http://localhost:3000/worlds/9011-new-cycle.wtml"
      proximity-criterion="Has the player successfully completed the first clean qualification test on the redesigned MJOLNIR drive and signed off on the test report?" />
    <rendering-seed>helix-dynamics-9007-vindicated-long-work-redesign-quiet-pride-new-normal</rendering-seed>
  </head>
  <body>
    <atmosphere>
      Lab 4, three months later. The overhead strips are still the same
      fluorescent white. The telemetry wall has been upgraded — two new
      monitors added, the resolution sharper. There's a new piece of
      hardware behind the pressure glass that didn't exist before:
      the redesigned confinement coil assembly, with the coupling
      correction Dr. Andersen specified. It is physically smaller
      than what it replaced. That still surprises you.
    </atmosphere>
    <societal-structure>
      The MJOLNIR program pause cost Helix Dynamics the penalty clause
      — 200 million credits — but the board's decision to halt rather
      than ship is now being framed, in the company's public communications,
      as "a demonstration of engineering integrity." Nobody who was in
      that conference room believes that framing entirely, but nobody
      disputes it either. The Meridian Colonial Fleet accepted a revised
      delivery schedule. The contract held. Vasquez kept her job.
      You got a salary review six weeks early.
    </societal-structure>
    <geography>
      Lab 4 is the same. Your workstation is in the same place. The
      break room coffee machine has been replaced — the new one makes
      worse coffee, which has become a running joke. The test chamber
      contains the redesigned drive. The test schedule on the wall
      shows Qualification Cycle 1 beginning in four hours.
    </geography>
    <general-description>
      The review board found it: a resonant coupling between the magnetic
      confinement coils and the exhaust manifold at specific ramp-down
      frequencies. Not catastrophic at test-bench power levels. At full
      operational power — the power levels a Colonial Fleet vessel would
      run in service — the modelling showed a 23% probability of coil
      fracture within 800 operating hours. Enough time to get crew
      into deep space. Not enough to get them back. The redesign took
      ten weeks. Dr. Andersen oversaw it. You were included in the
      technical working group. That was not standard for a Grade 2.
      Vasquez requested it. She has not explained why, and you haven't
      asked. You are four hours from the first clean test of a drive
      that you, partially, helped fix.
    </general-description>
    <npcs>
      Dr. Priya Vasquez: Something between you has shifted — not into
      friendship exactly, but into something more honest. She was not
      the villain of this story and she seems relieved that the story
      didn't need one. She is meticulous about the redesign. She will
      be standing at the telemetry wall with you in four hours.

      Dr. Freya Andersen (IESB): Still present, overseeing final
      qualification. Has been matter-of-fact about everything: the
      flaw, the fix, the test. She told you, once, in passing, that
      this is what the process is supposed to look like. She meant it
      as a compliment.

      Yuki Okafor: Senior Test Engineer. Was part of the redesign team.
      Is quieter than before — the Lab 7 connection (the prior incident
      in Lab 7 turned out to be an earlier, unlogged manifestation of
      the same flaw) has been referred to an internal ethics review.
      He is cooperating. He looks tired.
    </npcs>
    <user>
      <initial-location>Lab 4, Block A, Ring 1, Helix Dynamics Orbital Yard</initial-location>
      <initial-state>
        Three months in. Grade 2, technically, though your role has
        functionally expanded. The redesign is complete. First qualification
        test in four hours. You are the engineer of record for the overnight
        watch cycle that found the original anomaly. That is now a matter
        of official documentation.
      </initial-state>
      <description>
        The process worked. People got hurt by it in smaller ways — careers
        slowed, money lost, a contract delayed. But the drive didn't go
        into service with a flaw in it. You have four hours. The coffee
        is worse than it used to be. The test is going to be fine.
        You are fairly sure the test is going to be fine.
      </description>
    </user>
    <constraints>
      This is a resolution world, but not a triumphant one — the tone
      should be earned-quiet rather than celebratory. The player is not
      a hero; they did their job and the system, imperfectly, responded
      correctly. The Lab 7 thread (Ryo Sato, prior suppressed incident)
      is in the background — an unresolved ethical thread that the
      redesign didn't fully close. The coming test should feel genuinely
      significant. Not all tension is gone: first tests on redesigned
      hardware are always tense. But the dread is different from World 9005.
      This time, you know the data is right.
    </constraints>
  </body>
</wtml>
